WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:02.890
Like most things in Unreal,

2
00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:04.589
Unreal has got its own methods for casting

3
00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:07.269
with a lot of inbuilt checks and error catches in place

4
00:00:07.269 --> 00:00:09.250
to help engineers along the way.

5
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.490
Let's take a look at a few of the main types of cast

6
00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.330
you're likely to use

7
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.890
during the development of your project.

8
00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.890
Firstly, we've got the is a check

9
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:17.129
that will return a bool

10
00:00:17.129 --> 00:00:19.329
if this class is of a particular type.

11
00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:21.929
In this instance, is this class a fruit?

12
00:00:21.929 --> 00:00:23.289
Useful for if you want to make sure

13
00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.809
that we're dealing with the appropriate types

14
00:00:24.809 --> 00:00:28.059
without necessarily needing to access that type.

15
00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:30.500
From there, we've got a normal templated cast

16
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.340
that will convert this object into a pointer

17
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.439
of a templated type if possible.

18
00:00:34.439 --> 00:00:37.460
If this cast fails, a null pointer is returned.

19
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.399
This will call is a before proceeding

20
00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:41.710
to convert the pointer.

21
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.469
Next, we've got the exact cast for the template

22
00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:46.570
and it will convert this object to the templated type

23
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.670
but only if the types match exactly.

24
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.590
Lastly, we've got cast checked

25
00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:52.710
and this will convert the object

26
00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.229
into a point of a templated type

27
00:00:54.229 --> 00:00:56.070
but crash if it fails.

28
00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.890
It will call is a before proceeding to convert the pointer

29
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.670
but not in a shipping build.

30
00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:01.509
And as a result,

31
00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.329
the cast checked is slightly more performant cast

32
00:01:04.329 --> 00:01:06.590
outside of development environments.

33
00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.549
This cast can and should be used

34
00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:11.250
to explicitly convey the intention of the code.

35
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.950
For example, the behavior tree component

36
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.109
is married to the AI controller.

37
00:01:15.109 --> 00:01:16.670
So a cast checked is perfect

38
00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.269
for when retrieving the owner.

39
00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:20.590
Nothing else should be owning the behavior tree

40
00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.840
aside from the AI controller.

41
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.519
Of course, more standardized types of cast

42
00:01:24.519 --> 00:01:26.840
such as static, reinterpret and C style

43
00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.159
are all still available

44
00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:30.400
but 99% of the casts are typically done

45
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.099
using Unreal's casting system.
